\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A long symmetric torus is recovered at every site}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On the periodic cubical torus with both directions of every
axis and with every weight equal to #{1}, let #{N\ge 5}. Every
vertex is a \vocabk{recovery site}{fdip-00PQ} of rank #{n}.
For #{N=1} every difference is zero, so the affine functions
do not span a free module of positive rank. For #{N=2} the
vertex #{x-\varepsilon_i} equals #{x+\varepsilon_i}, and a
constant difference exists only when #{2a_i=0}. For #{N=3}
and for #{N=4}, every admissible set contains the star of
#{x}, and every edge of an axis cycle meets that star, so a
constant difference #{a_i} exists for every value in #{k}
only when #{Na_i=0}. Over #{\mathbb{Z}} the only slope is
#{0}, and no vertex is a recovery site. For these two values
of #{N}, if #{Na_i=0} for every #{a_i\in k}, then
\ref{fdip-00PK} makes every vertex a recovery site of rank
#{n}. The forward-edge torus has no backward edge, so the two
directions required by the \vocabk{recovery site}{fdip-00PQ}
are not both present. This is the obstruction of
\ref{fdip-00PE}. Deleting one outgoing edge at #{x} lowers
its out-degree by one and leaves another vertex at out-degree
#{2n}, so the rank is not constant on a set containing both.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{U} be the star of #{x}. Each displacement from #{x}
on an edge meeting #{U} lies in #{\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}}. For
#{N\ge 5} those 5 integers remain distinct in
#{\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}}, so the chart embeds. Then
#{f(x+m)=\sum_i a_i m_i} has difference #{a_i} on every chart
edge, for every #{a_i\in k}, and every edge meeting #{U} is a
chart edge. The odd vectors of \ref{fdip-00PO} form a free
basis of rank #{n}. Every weight is a unit, so #{\bar q} is
the standard dot form and \ref{fdip-00PC} gives
nondegeneracy. Both directions are present and the weights
agree. \ref{fdip-00PK} supplies the commutator square on the
cells whose vertices and one-step shifts lie in #{U},
including the vertex #{x}.}

\p{For #{N=1}, #{f(x+\varepsilon_i)-f(x)=0}. For #{N=2} the
two steps coincide, and the cycle sum is #{2a_i=0}. For
#{N=3}, #{x+2\varepsilon_i=x-\varepsilon_i}. For #{N=4},
#{x+2\varepsilon_i=x-2\varepsilon_i}. The star lies in every
admissible #{U}, so the cycle sum is #{Na_i=0}. Over
#{\mathbb{Z}} the slope is #{0}. If #{Na_i=0} for every
#{a_i}, the global function of \ref{fdip-00PK} hits every odd
vector and the square holds on the star. The forward-edge
torus has no backward generator. A deleted outgoing edge
leaves out-degrees #{2n} and #{2n-1}.}
}
